/**
 * The country itself.
 *
 * An Albers equal-area projection of the fifty states — the projection every
 * printed map of the United States uses, because it is the one that keeps
 * areas true: Texas is to Montana on the page what it is on the ground.
 * Alaska and Hawaii sit in the two insets a wall map has always put them in.
 *
 * The outlines come from the Census Bureau's public-domain boundary files,
 * projected once ahead of time and simplified to the point where the last
 * vertex removed stopped being visible at the size this is drawn. A whole
 * country in a little over thirty kilobytes, and no mapping library on the
 * page — the projection has already happened by the time this file exists.
 *
 * The two numbers on each state are its anchor: where the label sits and
 * where the tooltip points. That is the centroid, except where the centroid
 * falls outside the state — Michigan's lands in Lake Michigan, Hawaii's in
 * the Pacific — where it is the centre of the largest landmass instead.
 *
 * `labelled` is false where the state cannot hold two letters at this scale:
 * Rhode Island, Delaware, Connecticut, New Jersey, Vermont, New Hampshire,
 * Massachusetts, Maryland — and Hawaii, which is wide but is mostly ocean, and
 * a label has to sit on land or it is white type on the paper. They are still
 * filled, still hoverable, and still named on hover, which is most of the
 * reason the tooltip exists at all.
 */

